    description: 'Sean-nós (continuous tradition centuries-onward in Gaeltacht Irish-speaking regions of Connemara, Donegal, Kerry, and Cork, with continuous tradition through to present and revival from 1970s onward through Cumann Cheoltóirí Éireann and broader Irish-traditional-music-revival circuits), the Irish-Gaelic unaccompanied solo-vocal balladic tradition. Defined by highly-ornamented melismatic phrasing (the sean-nós specialty of intricate-decoration including grace-notes, mordents, slides, and characteristic regional ornament-conventions specific to Connemara, Donegal, and other Gaeltacht regions), modal-and-pentatonic melodic vocabulary drawing on Irish-traditional folk-modes, narrative ballad-and-love-song repertoire in Irish Gaelic language addressing emigration, romantic-loss, historical-narrative, and religious subjects, unaccompanied solo-vocal performance (no instrumental accompaniment in traditional sean-nós, though revival-era performances sometimes include unobtrusive drone), and family-and-community transmission through fireside-and-pub performance contexts. Distinguished from later Irish folk-revival singer-songwriter by retention of unaccompanied-traditional repertoire; distinguished from Scottish Gaelic puirt-a-beul by Irish-not-Scottish-Gaelic linguistic-and-musical lineage; distinguished from broader Anglo-Irish balladry by Irish-Gaelic-language commitment.',
