    axes: { harm: 1, pitch: 1, ornament: 1, meter: 1, density: 0, transmission: 1, improv: 0, soundTech: -1, intensity: 0, voice: 2, timbre: 0, percussion: -2, cyclicity: -2 },
    description: 'Fado de Coimbra (codified 19th century onward in Coimbra Portugal university-student community, with the Coimbra Academic Tradition\'s continuous transmission through to present), the Coimbra-university male-student fado tradition. Defined by Portuguese-guitar-and-classical-guitar duo accompaniment (the same instrumental palette as Lisbon fado but with Coimbra-specific tuning and stylistic conventions), male-student vocal performance (the Coimbra fado tradition is exclusively male-vocal, distinguishing from Lisbon\'s predominantly-female lead), university-student lyric content addressing academic life, Coimbra-city geographic-and-architectural features, romantic-melancholy, and Coimbra-specific cultural references, performance contexts of student-serenade in university courtyards and Coimbra night-time-streets and academic-festival celebrations, and traditional black-academic-cape costume during performance. Distinguished from Lisbon fado by male-student vocal exclusivity and university-student context; distinguished from broader Portuguese folk by university-academic specificity; distinguished from Brazilian or Cape Verdean fado-derivative genres by Portuguese-mainland and Coimbra-academic centrality.',
    exemplars: ['Coimbra University Tunas', 'Zeca Afonso', 'José Afonso'],
