    description: 'Greek éntechno (codified 1960 onward through Mikis Theodorakis and Manos Hadjidakis establishing the éntechno ("art") song tradition during late-Junta-era cultural ferment), the Greek art-song composer tradition. Defined by composer-driven settings of modern-Greek-poetry by Greek poets (Yannis Ritsos, Giorgos Seferis, Odysseas Elytis, Nikos Gatsos) to original melodic material, Greek-folk-derived modal-and-melodic vocabulary drawing on rebetiko and Eastern-Mediterranean modal traditions, instrumentation centered on bouzouki and laouto with guitar-and-piano-and-orchestra arrangement options, vocal delivery prioritizing literary-text-clarity over operatic projection, and performance contexts spanning concert hall, recording studio, and laiko (popular) theatrical venues. Distinguished from rebetiko by composer-not-folk-anonymous origin and literary-poetry-not-marginal-urban-text content; distinguished from later laiko by intellectual-and-literary register; distinguished from Greek folk by composed-not-traditional repertoire.',
