    description: 'The Mandarin yáolán qǔ (摇篮曲, literally "cradle-song") tradition continuous from imperial-era domestic-vocal practice through 20th-century revolutionary-era and post-reform-era domestic singing across mainland China, Taiwan, Singapore, and the broader Mandarin-Chinese-speaking diaspora; defined by Chinese pentatonic (gong-shang-jue-zhi-yu) melodic-vocabulary, narrow ambitus typically within a sixth, syllabic clear-melodic-line setting without melismatic ornament (contrasting with the Southwest-and-South-Chinese regional traditions which include more ornament), Mandarin-Putonghua literary-text register, intimate maternal-and-grandmother singing context. Distinguished from Cantonese cradle-song by Mandarin-not-Cantonese linguistic register and from Japanese komoriuta by Chinese-not-Japanese pentatonic-vocabulary inflection.',
    exemplars: ['"Yáolán qǔ" 摇篮曲 (traditional, canonical)', '"Mā mā gē" 妈妈歌 (Mother song, cradle-adjacent)', '"Bǎo bao kuài shuì" 宝宝快睡 (modern traditional)', 'Hua Liu / Teresa Teng cradle-song recordings', 'Northwest-Chinese folk cradle-song (Shaanxi xintianyou-adjacent)'],
