    description: 'The Persian aroosi (عروسی, wedding) music tradition continuous from medieval Persian-domestic-music practice through Qajar-and-Pahlavi-era documented performance into modern Iranian and Iranian-diaspora practice; defined by daf (Persian frame-drum) and tar/setar (Persian long-necked lutes) and tonbak (goblet-drum) ensemble accompaniment to vocal-foreground celebratory songs, Persian dastgāh-modal vocabulary inflected toward the lighter Shur and Bayāt-e Tork gushehs appropriate to wedding-affect (avoiding the gravitas of Mahur or the lament-leaning Dashti), 6/8 reng-dance and 4/4 tasnif-song forms canonical for wedding repertoire, Farsi-Persian celebratory text register, henna-night and bandari-coastal-tradition variants. The Mohammad Reza Lotfi and Hossein Alizadeh classical-context recordings provide the canonical art-music elevation; the broader Tehrani pre-revolutionary popular-vocalist wedding-repertoire is the more-domestic continuous tradition.',
