    description: 'The Yoruba wedding-music tradition continuous from pre-colonial Yoruba ritual-practice through 19th-20th century continuous performance into modern Yoruba-and-Yoruba-diaspora domestic practice across southwestern Nigeria, Benin, Togo, and the Atlantic Afro-diaspora; the aso ebi (unified-dress) wedding tradition emphasizes group-celebration vocal-and-drumming participation where extended-family-and-friends-of-the-couple wear matching cloth and participate in the celebration as unified social-group. Defined by talking-drum (dùndún) and dunun-and-bata percussion-foreground ensemble, Yoruba tonal-language vocal-melody constrained by speech-tone patterns, call-and-response group-vocal foreground, ìjò Yoruba dance-rhythmic-vocabulary, Yoruba-language ojúfò (wedding-praise) and oríkì (praise-poetry) text-tradition. Modern Yoruba wedding integrates juju and fuji popular-music traditions alongside traditional aso ebi practice.',
    exemplars: ['Sunny Adé — Yoruba juju wedding-music canonical', 'Ebenezer Obey — Yoruba juju wedding-repertoire', 'King Wasiu Ayinde Marshal — fuji wedding-music', 'Traditional Yoruba aso ebi wedding-procession songs', 'Ọlatúnjí Adégbọye — Yoruba traditional-wedding documentation'],
