    description: 'The Andean Quechua-and-Aymara wedding-music tradition continuous from pre-Columbian Andean ritual-practice through colonial-period syncretic continuous performance into modern Quechua-and-Aymara-speaking domestic practice across Peru, Bolivia, Ecuador, and the Andean diaspora; the huayno (Quechua wayñu, Aymara waynu) is the canonical Andean dance-form across multiple ceremonial contexts including weddings. Defined by charango (small ten-string lute) and quena (Andean notched-flute) and zampoña (panpipes) and accordion (post-colonial addition) instrumental ensemble accompanying voice, Andean pentatonic-modal vocabulary, characteristic 2/4 huayno rhythmic-foundation with the canonical "tan-takataka" foot-pattern, Quechua-and-Aymara-and-Spanish text register, call-and-response group-vocal participation, festive dance-foreground spanning multi-hour wedding-feast performance.',
    exemplars: ['Inkuyo — Quechua wedding-huayno traditional recordings', 'Los Kjarkas — Bolivian wedding-huayno popular', 'Picaflor de los Andes — Peruvian wedding-huayno canonical', 'Pastorita Huaracina — Peruvian wedding-vocal master', 'Andean matrimonio-huayno-traditional-procession ensemble repertoire'],
