    axes: { harm: -1, pitch: 1, ornament: 1, meter: 0, density: 0, transmission: -2, improv: 1, soundTech: -2, intensity: 0, voice: 2, timbre: 0, percussion: 0, cyclicity: 1 },
    description: 'The Algerian raï tradition from its 1920s-1930s Oran-region origin as Bedouin-Andalusian women\'s-tradition (the cheikha women-singers performing in cafe-and-cabaret contexts the political-and-erotic-and-anti-clerical raï repertoire considered scandalous by both colonial-French and Islamic-conservative-Algerian authorities) through 1970s-1980s pre-commercial cheikha-and-cheb politicization era through 1990s commercial-pop-raï crossover. Defined by Arabic-maqam modal vocabulary (Bayāti, Saba), oud-and-darbuka-and-accordion-and-violin ensemble accompaniment in pre-commercial form, melismatic Algerian-Arabic vocal foreground, characteristic vocal-foreground arrangement, Algerian-Arabic-and-French text register, political-anti-clerical-and-social-grievance lyric content in pre-commercial form. The political-and-anti-clerical content of pre-commercial raï made it a target of both Algerian-state censorship and Islamist-fundamentalist violence — Cheb Hasni was assassinated September 1994.',
