    description: 'The Vietnamese nhạc phản chiến ("anti-war music") protest-song tradition continuous from 1960s-1975 Vietnam-War-era South-Vietnam political-songwriter scene continuous through post-1975 reunification-era continuous performance (though heavily restricted under unified-Vietnam government for songs critical of either side of the conflict); centered on Trịnh Công Sơn (1939-2001, the most-iconic Vietnamese songwriter whose "Nối Vòng Tay Lớn" 1969 anti-war anthem and broader anti-war catalog made him simultaneously banned by both the South-Vietnam-republic and post-1975-North-Vietnam-government for periods), Phạm Duy, Khánh Ly (Trịnh Công Sơn\'s canonical vocalist). Defined by acoustic-guitar-and-voice songwriter-format with Western-folk stylistic inheritance crossed with Vietnamese-traditional-pentatonic melodic vocabulary, Vietnamese-language tonal-language-melody-locked vocal-melody-constrained-by-speech-tone, anti-war-and-anti-violence-and-humanist lyric content rather than explicitly partisan-leftist-or-anti-Communist framing, intimate acoustic timbre.',
