    description: 'The Māori praise-chant tradition continuous from pre-contact Māori ritual-practice through 19th-20th century continuous performance at marae context into modern Māori-cultural-revival; patere (women\'s rapid-recitation praise-and-genealogy-chant typically performed in response to slights or insults requiring whakapapa-defense) and pao (impromptu praise-song often two-line couplet praise-improvisations) are the canonical Māori praise-chant forms within the broader waiata mōteatea (chant-song) category. Defined by narrow-range pentatonic-leaning melodic-vocabulary characteristic of Māori-traditional vocal practice, free-meter rapid-paced recitation, te-reo-Māori text register with extensive whakapapa (genealogy) and praise-and-historical-narrative content, unaccompanied solo-or-group vocal performance, ritual-bounded performance context at marae.',
    exemplars: ['Hirini Melbourne — traditional waiata mōteatea recordings 1980s-1990s', 'Patere repertoire documented by Apirana Ngata (Ngā Mōteatea Vol I-IV collection)', 'Whirimako Black — modern Māori praise-chant revival', 'Tūhoe tribal waiata mōteatea corpus (Best 1925 ethnography)', 'Modern marae patere-and-pao continuous-living tradition'],
