    description: 'The English-language nursery-rhyme tradition continuous from medieval and early-modern English play-song origins through 18th-century chapbook publication (Tommy Thumb\'s Pretty Song Book 1744 the earliest documented English nursery-rhyme collection; Mother Goose\'s Melody 1765 codifying many canonical rhymes) through Iona and Peter Opie\'s 20th-century Lore and Language of Schoolchildren ethnographic documentation establishing the durability of oral-transmission across British-and-American-childhood generations. Defined by narrow-range pentatonic-or-hexatonic melodic vocabulary, repeating short melodic cells durable across centuries (the "Mary Had a Little Lamb" tune is the documented first sound-recorded human voice on Edison\'s 1877 tinfoil cylinder), action-coordination structure for hand-game and circle-game and counting-out applications, English-language plain-text register, unaccompanied voice as primary performance medium.',
    exemplars: ['"Mary Had a Little Lamb" 1830 Sarah Josepha Hale text', '"Ring Around the Rosie" (Iona and Peter Opie documentation)', '"Hot Cross Buns" early-19th-century London street-cry', '"Twinkle Twinkle Little Star" 1806 Jane Taylor text (on French "Ah! vous dirai-je, maman" tune)', '"London Bridge Is Falling Down" medieval-origin English ring-game'],
