    description: 'The West-African Senegambian-Mande rice-planting song tradition continuous from premodern Senegambian-and-Guinean rice-agriculture village-labor practice (the Sahelian-rice-belt of modern Senegal, Gambia, Guinea-Bissau, Sierra Leone and the rice-growing-knowledge that traveled with enslaved-Africans transported to the South-Carolina-Georgia Lowcountry) through colonial-period continuous performance into modern West-African-rural continuous practice. The West-African Senegambian rice-planting song tradition was demonstrably the documented lineage origin point for the South-Carolina Lowcountry rice-cultivation work-songs continuous into the broader American-South work-song tradition (per Judith Carney Black Rice 2001 canonical-scholarly-documentation). Defined by kora-and-voice arrangement (in modern documented context), Manding-Maninka-Wolof text register, Mande-pentatonic modal vocabulary, characteristic rice-paddy task-coordination structure.',
    exemplars: ['Modern Senegambian rice-paddy continuous-living tradition', 'Judith Carney — Black Rice 2001 (canonical scholarly documentation)', 'Sierra Leone Mende rice-cultivation song traditional', 'Guinea-Bissau Balanta rice-paddy song traditional', 'Senegambian rice-planting song continuous-tradition'],
