    description: 'The Mongolian-and-Tuvan hunting-khoomei (throat-singing) tradition continuous from premodern Central-Asian-steppe hunter-and-herder practice through 20th-century Soviet-era continuous performance into modern Mongolian-and-Tuvan continuous practice; khoomei (the canonical Mongolian-Tuvan multiphonic-overtone-singing where the singer produces simultaneously a fundamental-low-drone and a high-melodic-whistle from a single vocal-source) is documented as having hunting-and-pastoral origins in the steppe-and-taiga environment; the khoomei was historically practiced by herder-hunter-shamans to imitate natural-environment sounds (wind, rivers, animals, mountains) and to communicate-with or hunt-call game. Defined by unaccompanied solo-vocal overtone-multiphonic arrangement, Mongolian-or-Tuvan-language text register, characteristic drone-overtone vocal-production, hunting-magic and pastoral-imitation performance-context.',
