    description: 'The Andean Quechua-and-Aymara spinning-and-weaving song tradition continuous from pre-Hispanic Andean-textile labor practice (the Andean textile-tradition is documented as among the most-elaborate in pre-Columbian Americas with extensive women\'s-textile-labor song-coordination; the canonical pre-Hispanic Inca-era cumbi-and-ahuasca textile-tradition involved hereditary aclla-cuna textile-women performing labor-coordination song) through Spanish-colonial-era continuous performance into modern Quechua-and-Aymara-speaking Andean-rural continuous practice. The Andean spinning-song integrates extended drop-spindle and back-strap-loom weaving with multi-hour song-performance, with characteristic intimate-melancholic style distinct from the festive huayno-de-trabajo work-song tradition. Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-solo vocal arrangement, Quechua-Aymara-and-Spanish text register, Andean-pentatonic modal vocabulary.',
    exemplars: ['Modern Andean-village spinning-and-weaving continuous-living tradition', 'Quechua women-spinning canonical traditional', 'Aymara women-weaving canonical traditional (Bolivia)', 'Inca-era aclla-cuna textile-tradition canonical-historical', 'Andean drop-spindle and back-strap-loom continuous-tradition'],
