    description: 'The Bengali jata gita (grinding-mill song) and broader Indian-subcontinent grinding-and-millstone song tradition continuous from premodern South-Asian domestic-grain-processing labor practice (women-laborers performed evening-and-pre-dawn grain-grinding at the stone-grinding-mill jata with extensive song-coordination; the jata gita is documented across Bengali, Maharashtrian, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, and broader Indian-rural domestic-labor contexts) through 19th-20th century continuous performance into modern Bengali-Bangladeshi-and-broader-Indian continuous practice. The Maharashtrian Marathi ovi (grinding-song verse) tradition is among the most-documented variants; the Pune-based GRINDmill Songs Project has documented over 100,000 women\'s grinding-mill song verses since 1990s. Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-solo vocal arrangement, regional-Indian-language text register, characteristic Hindustani-folk modal vocabulary.',
    exemplars: ['Maharashtrian Marathi ovi (grinding-song) tradition canonical', 'GRINDmill Songs Project Pune 1990s-onward canonical documentation', 'Bengali jata gita continuous-living tradition', 'Karnataka and Tamil Nadu women\'s grinding-song traditional', 'Bahinabai Chaudhari — canonical Marathi peasant-poet (broader ovi tradition)'],
