    description: 'The Wolof-Senegambian women\'s grain-grinding song tradition continuous from premodern Senegambian domestic-grain-processing labor practice (the millet-and-sorghum and rice grain-grinding context where women performed extended labor with mortar-and-pestle pounding; the canonical Senegambian women\'s pestle-pounding rhythm with two-or-three women alternating-strokes on a single mortar is documented as foundational to broader West-African polyrhythm) through colonial-era continuous performance into modern Senegambian continuous practice. The grain-grinding-song tradition integrates pestle-pounding rhythmic-coordination with extensive group-vocal song (the Senegambian women\'s mortar-and-pestle two-or-three-woman alternating-stroke pattern is also the documented origin point for some Afro-diasporic Caribbean percussion-traditions). Defined by call-and-response group-vocal arrangement with pestle-pounding rhythmic-foundation, Wolof-Mandinka-and-broader-Senegambian text register, characteristic Mande-pentatonic modal vocabulary.',
    exemplars: ['Wolof women\'s grain-pounding continuous-living tradition', 'Mandinka women\'s rice-pounding canonical traditional', 'Senegambian women\'s pestle-pounding polyrhythm canonical-Afro-diasporic-origin', 'Modern Senegambian rural-village domestic-labor continuous-tradition', 'Hugh Tracey early-20th-century West-African field-recordings canonical scholarly'],
